"texte": [
"For generations, the pursuit of an ever-higher standard of living has served as the undisputed engine of modern progress. Economists measure national success through Gross Domestic Product, while individuals frequently equate personal advancement with larger homes, faster vehicles, and cutting-edge luxuries. Undoubtedly, raising material living standards has lifted millions out of destitution, extended human life expectancy, and eradicated numerous preventable diseases. Yet, once basic physical and financial needs are comfortably met, does the relentless quest for material upgrades continue to enhance human well-being, or does it eventually become counterproductive?",
"Proponents of continuous economic and material growth argue that human aspiration is inherently boundless. From this perspective, striving for greater comfort drives technological innovation, generates employment, and expands personal freedom. Having greater financial resources enables individuals to access superior healthcare, travel broadly, and invest in education. Consequently, halting the pursuit of material improvement could lead to economic stagnation and diminished opportunities for future generations.",
"Nevertheless, a growing body of psychological and ecological evidence challenges this assumption. Economists refer to the 'hedonic treadmill'—the tendency of humans to quickly adapt to improved material circumstances, causing the initial thrill of a higher income or luxury purchase to fade rapidly. As expectations rise alongside income, individuals find themselves working longer hours simply to maintain a lifestyle that no longer brings them genuine joy. Furthermore, on a planet with finite resources, the universal pursuit of boundless material consumption is ecologically unsustainable, accelerating climate disruption and biodiversity loss.",
