"titre": "The Influence of Family Background on Academic Success",
"titre2": "Exercice b019 (Semi-dialogue / Academic Interview)",
"texte": [
"Interviewer: Welcome to 'Social Lens.' Today we’re speaking with sociologist Dr. Nadia Koury about a persistent reality in education: the powerful link between family background and academic achievement. Dr. Koury, despite decades of school reforms, why does socioeconomic status remain such a strong predictor of educational outcomes?",
"Dr. Koury: Thank you. The primary reason is that learning does not begin or end at the schoolhouse gate. Sociologists refer to 'cultural capital'—the subtle habits, vocabulary, attitudes toward reading, and familiarity with institutional expectations that parents pass on to their children. When middle-class or affluent parents read bedtime stories, visit museums, or coach their children on how to negotiate with teachers, those students enter school already fluent in the unspoken rules of academic success.",
"Interviewer: Does that mean financial wealth is the sole factor determining whether a child thrives academically?",
"Dr. Koury: Not exclusively, although financial stability certainly alleviates chronic stress and buys resources like quiet study spaces or private tutoring. However, parental engagement and emotional support play an equally decisive role. Even in low-income households, when parents express high expectations, show genuine curiosity about schoolwork, and foster a resilient attitude toward mistakes, children frequently defy statistical odds. Conversely, intense parental pressure focused solely on prestigious grades can backfire, triggering burnout and anxiety regardless of family income.",
"Interviewer: How can public schools counterbalance these disparities so that a child’s destiny isn't predetermined by their parents' background?",
"Dr. Koury: Schools must actively bridge the gap rather than assuming all families possess identical resources. Providing free after-school mentoring, lending books and digital devices, and welcoming working-class parents as genuine partners—without judgment—can transform school culture. Ultimately, while we cannot equalize every home environment, we can ensure that schools amplify every student’s potential rather than merely rewarding inherited privilege."
