"titre": "Do New Technologies Make Individuals More Independent?",
"titre2": "Exercice b035 (Semi-dialogue / Philosophy Podcast)",
"texte": [
"Host: Welcome to 'Modern Paradox.' Today we’re joined by philosopher of technology Dr. Julian Thorne to examine an intriguing question: do modern digital technologies genuinely make individuals more independent, or do they quietly foster new forms of dependency? Dr. Thorne, on the surface, we seem more empowered than ever.",
"Dr. Thorne: Indeed, the empowerment is undeniable. With a single pocket device, an individual today can navigate an unfamiliar foreign city without a paper guide, manage international investments without a broker, translate languages instantaneously, and acquire complex practical skills via online tutorials. In many respects, digital tools have dismantled traditional gatekeepers, granting ordinary people unprecedented self-sufficiency in daily life.",
"Host: Yet you refer to this as a paradox. Where does the hidden dependency emerge?",
"Dr. Thorne: It emerges the moment we examine what happens when those digital infrastructures falter—or how much cognitive effort we outsource to them. Because we rely perpetually on GPS navigation, many drivers struggle to form mental maps of their own neighborhoods. Similarly, spell-checkers, predictive text, and generative AI risk atrophying our capacity for independent writing and critical memory if used uncritically. Even more profoundly, our autonomy is subtly constrained by invisible software ecosystems: when a few dominant platforms control our cloud storage, payment systems, and communication channels, we are deeply dependent on corporate servers we neither own nor understand.",
"Host: So convenience comes at the price of self-reliance. How can individuals enjoy technological efficiency without surrendering their autonomy?",
"Dr. Thorne: Through intentional discernment. Technology enhances independence when it acts as a bicycle for the mind—amplifying human capability rather than replacing foundational skills. By deliberately practicing unaided navigation, deep reading, and independent problem-solving alongside smart tools, we ensure that we remain capable agents rather than helpless passengers of our devices."
