"titre": "Can Robots Replace Human Beings in Certain Fields?",
"titre2": "Exercice b036 (Speech / Discours)",
"texte": [
"Members of the Innovation Council, engineers, and healthcare professionals,",
"Over the past decade, robotics has leaped dramatically beyond the confines of automotive assembly lines. Today, autonomous machines harvest delicate crops, assist surgeons with sub-millimeter precision, deliver medications across hospital corridors, and even serve as interactive companions in eldercare facilities. Consequently, a pressing question confronts both policymakers and the public: in which domains can—and should—robots legitimately replace human beings, and where does human presence remain ethically and practically irreplaceable?",
"On the one hand, there are numerous fields where robotic substitution represents an unambiguous triumph for human safety and efficiency. Environments characterized by extreme danger—such as deep-sea exploration, nuclear disaster cleanup, mining, and firefighting—expose human workers to lethal hazards that machines can endure without risk to life. Similarly, in precision manufacturing, warehouse logistics, and microsurgery, robotic systems operate without fatigue, emotional distraction, or physical tremor, dramatically reducing error rates and saving lives.",
"On the other hand, assuming that technical capability automatically justifies human replacement overlooks the profound relational dimensions of many professions. Consider education, psychotherapy, nursing, and eldercare. While a humanoid robot can dispense pills on schedule or answer factual questions patiently, it cannot experience genuine empathy, comprehend unspoken emotional grief, or offer the comforting warmth of authentic human solidarity. When vulnerable patients or developing children are entrusted primarily to machines simply to cut labor costs, society risks trading compassionate care for cold mechanical efficiency.",
"Ultimately, the central question is not merely what robots can do, but what kind of society we wish to inhabit. Automation should be enthusiastically deployed to liberate humans from hazardous, grueling, or repetitive tasks. However, in professions rooted in moral judgment, emotional connection, and human dignity, robots must remain collaborative assistants rather than substitutes. By reserving care, mentorship, and ethical responsibility for human beings, we ensure that technological progress serves humanity rather than hollowing it out. Thank you."
